\[
P(k \text{ heads}) = \binom{n}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^k \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{n-k} = \binom{n}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n
\]

Here, $n = 12$ and the probability of success (getting heads) is $\frac{1}{2}$.

1. Probability of getting 9 heads:
\[
P(9 \text{ heads}) = \binom{12}{9} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 4940 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

2. Probability of getting 10 heads:
\[
P(10 \text{ heads}) = \binom{12}{10} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 360 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

3. Probability of getting 11 heads:
\[
P(10 \text{ heads}) = \binom{12}{11} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 12 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

4. Probability of getting 12 heads:
\[
P(12 \text{ heads}) = \binom{12}{12} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 1 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

Adding these probabilities together:
\[
P(\text{at least 9 heads}) = P(9 \text{ heads}) + P(10 \text{ heads}) + P(11 \text{ heads}) + P(12 \text{ heads})
\]
\[
= 4940 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} + 360 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} + 12 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} + 1 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]
\[
= (4940 + 360 + 12 + 1) \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 5313 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

Calculating the value:
\[
5313 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = \frac{5313}{2048} \approx 2.63
\]

The correct calculation is:
\[
\boxed{\frac{5313}{2048}} \approx 0.263
\]
