\mathbf{A} \mathbf{x} = \lambda \mathbf{A}_c \mathbf{x}
\]

where:  
- \(\mathbf{x}\) is the vector of displacements for the blocks,  
- \(\mathbf{A}\) is the diagonal matrix of the system's covariance matrices,  
- \(\mathbf{A}_c\) is the cross-covariance matrix, and  
- \(\lambda\) is the eigenvalue (eigen value) of the system.

For \( N = 3 \) blocks, the system is:

\[

\left( \begin{array}{c} m_t \mathbf{A}_1 \\ m_t \mathbf{A}_2 \\ m_t \mathbf{A}_3 \end{array} \right) \mathbf{x} = \lambda \left( \begin{array}{c} \mathbf{A}_c_1 \\ \mathbf{A_c_2} \\ \mathbf{A}_c_3 \end{array} \right) \mathbf{x}
\]

The diagonal matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is:

\[
\mathbf{A} = \left( \begin{array}{ccc} k_t & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & k_t & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & k_t \end{bmatrix} \right)
\]

The covariance matrix \(\mathbf{A}_c\) is:

\[
\mathbf{A}_c = \left( \begin{array}{ccc} 0 & K_c & K_c \\ K_k & 0 & K_k \\ K_k & K_k & 0 \end{bmatrix} \right)
\]

